<asp:Content ID="Content3" ContentPlaceHolderID="ContentPlaceHolder2" runat="server">
    
    <div id="MainBox">
        <h2>
            Gohan, the son of Goku and Chi-Chi, is one of the most complex and dynamic characters in the <br>
            Dragon Ball series. His journey from a shy, timid child to one of the most powerful warriors <br>
            in the universe is filled with emotional depth and personal growth, making him one of the most <br>
            beloved characters in the franchise. Unlike his father, whose love for battle defines much of his <br>
            character, Gohan’s story revolves around balancing the expectations placed upon him as a fighter <br>
            with his own desire for peace and intellectual pursuits.
        </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="textBoxes" id="box1">
        <h2>
            Gohan’s introduction in Dragon Ball Z marks the beginning of his transformative journey. As a <br>
            young boy, Gohan is sheltered and overprotected by his mother, Chi-Chi, who wishes for him to <br>
            focus on his studies and become a scholar. From an early age, Chi-Chi is adamant about Gohan leading <br>
            a peaceful, academic life, unlike his father, Goku, who thrives on fighting and adventure. As a <br>
            result, Gohan starts out as a timid and fearful child, unaccustomed to the harsh realities of combat <br>
            that define the world around him.
        </h2>
        <img id="kidGohanBaby" src="/images/kidGohanBaby.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box2">
        <h2>
            However, Gohan’s latent power is hinted at from the very beginning. Despite his tender age and <br>
            innocent demeanor, Gohan possesses incredible potential as a fighter. This potential is first <br>
            demonstrated during the Saiyan Saga when Raditz, Goku’s brother, kidnaps Gohan. In a fit of <br>
            rage after witnessing his father in peril, Gohan unleashes a powerful energy attack, breaking <br>
            Raditz’s armor. This moment foreshadows Gohan’s incredible hidden strength, which becomes a key <br>
            theme throughout the series.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box3">
        <h2>
            Gohan’s character truly begins to develop during the Saiyan Saga. After Raditz’s defeat, Goku <br>
            dies and Piccolo takes Gohan under his wing, recognizing the boy's potential. Piccolo’s relationship <br>
            with Gohan is one of the most significant in the series, as the stern Namekian warrior becomes a mentor <br>
            and father figure to him. Though their initial relationship is strained—Piccolo abducts Gohan to train <br>
            him without Chi-Chi’s consent—the two form a deep bond over time. Piccolo’s tough love helps Gohan grow <br>
            both as a fighter and as a person. During their training, Gohan gradually overcomes his fear and learns <br>
            to harness his inner strength.
        </h2>
        <img id="GohanPiccolo2" src="/images/GohanPiccolo2.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box4">
        <h2>
            When Vegeta and Nappa arrive on Earth, Gohan is forced into battle far earlier than he is ready <br>
            for. Despite his fear, Gohan demonstrates his potential again, delivering powerful blows and fighting <br>
            alongside Earth’s greatest warriors. Though still young and hesitant, Gohan steps up when necessary, <br>
            most notably during the climactic battle against Vegeta. When Goku is incapacitated, Gohan transforms <br>
            into his Great Ape form and contributes significantly to the Saiyan prince’s defeat. This battle marks <br>
            the first time Gohan actively fights to protect those he loves, even if he’s not yet fully confident in <br>
            his abilities.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box5">
        <h2>
            The Namek Saga further develops Gohan’s role as a fighter. After the battle with Vegeta, Gohan, <br>
            along with Krillin and Bulma, travels to Namek to find the Dragon Balls and revive their fallen <br>
            friends. Despite the escalating danger on Namek, Gohan repeatedly shows his bravery, standing up <br>
            against powerful foes like the Ginyu Force and Frieza’s elite henchmen. His courage is especially <br>
            evident when he defies the tyrannical Frieza, going so far as to launch an attack on the villain <br>
            to protect his friends and the Namekian people. It is during this time that Gohan’s compassionate <br>
            and empathetic nature shines through. He is not motivated by a love of battle, but by a desire to <br>
            protect the innocent and bring peace to the universe.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box6">
        <h2>
            The Android and Cell Sagas mark Gohan’s most significant arc, showcasing his growth from a <br>
            reluctant fighter into one of Earth’s greatest protectors. After years of peace following the defeat <br>
            of Frieza, Gohan has returned to a more normal life, focusing on his studies as his mother always <br>
            wanted. However, the arrival of Dr. Gero’s androids and the threat of Cell forces Gohan back into the <br>
            world of combat.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box7">
        <h2>
            Goku, recognizing Gohan’s latent power, begins training his son in the Hyperbolic Time Chamber <br>
            to prepare him for the upcoming battle with Cell. Unlike his father, who thrives on the thrill of <br>
            battle, Gohan still struggles with the idea of fighting. He is gentle by nature and dislikes the <br>
            violence that comes with being a warrior. However, Goku believes that Gohan’s true potential can <br>
            only be unlocked in the heat of battle, and he pushes his son to his limits during their training.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box8">
        <h2>
            The culmination of Gohan’s development occurs during the Cell Games. Goku shocks everyone by surrendering 
            his fight with Cell and nominating Gohan to take his place, fully confident that his son’s hidden power 
            will be enough to defeat the bio-engineered android. Initially, Gohan is hesitant and overwhelmed, unable 
            to match Cell’s strength. But as the battle intensifies, Cell’s sadistic cruelty triggers something deep 
            within Gohan. When Android 16 is killed in front of him, Gohan finally unleashes his full power, transforming 
            into Super Saiyan 2 in one of the most iconic moments in the series.
        </h2>
        <img id="kidGohan1" src="/images/kidGohan1.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box9">
        <h2>
            In this form, Gohan is far more powerful than Cell, dominating the villain in battle. However, his <br>
            newfound power also brings a sense of arrogance and overconfidence. Gohan begins toying with Cell, <br>
            prolonging the fight instead of ending it quickly. This decision ultimately leads to Goku’s death, <br>
            as Goku sacrifices himself to save the Earth from Cell’s self-destruction. The guilt of his father’s <br>
            death weighs heavily on Gohan, but he ultimately redeems himself by defeating Cell once and for all <br>
            with a powerful Kamehameha wave, with the spirit of Goku guiding him from the afterlife.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box10">
        <h2>
            The Cell Saga solidifies Gohan’s place as Earth’s strongest warrior, but it also highlights his <br>
            internal conflict. While Gohan has the potential to surpass even his father, his gentle nature <br>
            makes him reluctant to embrace the life of a warrior. He fights when he must, but he does not <br>
            seek out battle in the same way that Goku does. This inner conflict defines much of Gohan’s character <br>
            going forward.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box11">
        <h2>
            Following the Cell Games, Gohan steps away from the world of fighting to focus on his studies, <br>
            as his mother had always wished. By the time of the Buu Saga, Gohan is a teenager attending high <br>
            school, trying to live a normal life. He adopts the superhero persona of the Great Saiyaman to fight <br>
            crime in Satan City, but his days of intense combat seem to be behind him.
        </h2>
        <img id="greatsaiyaman1" src="/images/greatsaiyaman1.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box12">
        <h2>
            However, Gohan is once again drawn back into the world of fighting when the wizard Babidi begins <br>
            his plot to revive Majin Buu. Though Gohan has not been training as rigorously as before, he still <br>
            possesses immense power, especially after receiving training from the Supreme Kai and unlocking his <br>
            “Mystic” or “Ultimate” form. In this state, Gohan’s power rivals that of even the strongest fighters <br>
            in the universe, without the need to transform into a Super Saiyan.
        </h2>
        <img id="GohanUltimate1" src="/images/GohanUltimate1.png">
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box13">
        <h2>
            Despite his newfound strength, Gohan’s reluctance to fully embrace his role as a warrior once again 
            hinders him. During his battle with Super Buu, Gohan initially dominates the fight but becomes overconfident, 
            much like he did during the Cell Games. This overconfidence allows Buu to absorb him, leading to the eventual 
            need for Goku and Vegeta to step in and save the day.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box14">
        <h2>
            While Gohan plays a significant role in the Buu Saga, his character arc during this period is <br>
            more about his struggle to balance his responsibilities as a protector of Earth with his desire <br>
            for a peaceful life. By the end of the saga, Gohan has married Videl and started a family of his <br>
            own, further distancing himself from the world of combat.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box15">
        <h2>
            Dragon Ball Super provides Gohan with new opportunities for growth, though his role is more subdued <br>
            compared to earlier arcs. At the beginning of Super, Gohan has fully embraced his life as a scholar <br>
            and family man, focusing on raising his daughter, Pan, and continuing his studies. He has grown <br>
            weaker due to lack of training, and his absence from the battlefield is noticeable during key moments, <br>
            such as the battle against Beerus and the resurrection of Frieza.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box16">
        <h2>
            However, Gohan’s desire to protect his family and friends remains as strong as ever, and this <br>
            is what ultimately pulls him back into training. During the Tournament of Power, Gohan once again <br>
            steps up as a key member of Universe 7’s team. Though he is no longer the strongest fighter in the <br>
            universe, Gohan’s strategic mind and leadership skills make him an invaluable asset to the team. He <br>
            forms a strong partnership with Piccolo once again, and the two work together to eliminate powerful <br>
            opponents.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box17">
        <h2>
            Gohan’s participation in the Tournament of Power marks a return to form for his character. Though <br>
            he may never fully embrace the life of a warrior, Gohan proves that he is still a force to be <br>
            reckoned with when it comes to protecting his loved ones. His ability to balance his responsibilities <br>
            as a scholar, husband, father, and fighter makes him one of the most well-rounded characters in the series.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box18">
        <h2>
            Gohan’s journey throughout Dragon Ball Z and Super is one of personal growth, sacrifice, and self-discovery.<br>
            Unlike his father, Goku, who thrives on the thrill of battle, Gohan is driven by a desire to protect <br>
            those he loves and maintain peace. His reluctance to embrace the warrior’s path makes him a more relatable <br>
            and complex character, as he struggles to reconcile his immense power with his gentle nature.
        </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="textBoxes" id="box19">
        <h2>
            From his early days as a frightened child to his transformation into one of the universe’s strongest <br>
            warriors, Gohan’s evolution is one of the most compelling arcs in the Dragon Ball series. While he <br>
            may never fully embrace the life of a fighter, his strength, intelligence, and compassion make him a <br>
            true hero in every sense of the word.
        </h2>
    </div>

